@h1 Styling h1@

Wit gives you a good-looking document with zero effort, a light touch for small
tweaks, and a full escape hatch when you want to art-direct everything. Three
levels: the batteries-included theme, small overrides layered on top, or a raw
base you build up from nothing.

@h2 The batteries-included theme h2@

Out of the box, a built document looks like a clean word-processor page: a
familiar sans-serif body face, a comfortable reading size, and an
eight-and-a-half-inch "page" with an inch of margin sitting on a soft grey
canvas. It adapts to a reader's dark-mode preference, and it carries print rules
so a PDF paginates sensibly — margins set, and headings, figures, and tables kept
from breaking awkwardly across pages. You get all of this without writing a line
of CSS.

@h2 Small tweaks over the theme h2@

To adjust one thing — an accent colour, say — override a theme variable with a
style block. It cascades _over_ the theme, changing just what you name:

@@@pre
@@style
.wit-doc { --wit-accent: #2b6a4f; }
style@@
pre@@@

The theme scopes its rules to the document's own class, so keep your overrides
scoped there too and they will land as you expect.

@h2 Interpolating a value into a style h2@

Inside a raw style block you can pull a value from a record with a double-brace
interpolation — handy when a colour or size lives in your data:

@@@pre
@@style
.wit-doc { --wit-accent: {{theme.accent}}; }
style@@
pre@@@

@aside(class note) 💡 One thing to know: this double-brace interpolation is live
_only_ inside a raw block's body or string — not in ordinary prose. In running
text it stays literal. To put a value into your prose, define it and reference it
by name instead. aside@

@h2 The raw base — total control h2@

When you want to design the whole thing yourself, build with the raw base. It
strips the theme down to a mechanical reset — the box model, no body margin, a
little responsive-media safety — and hands you a blank canvas:

@@@pre
wit build essay.wit --raw
pre@@@

From there you style everything with your own style blocks and wrapping nodes —
a generic container with a class, a section — exactly as you would a hand-built
page. The same CSS drives HTML and PDF, so your print rules and page layout carry
straight into the PDF output.

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li _Expecting a double-brace or a capture to interpolate in prose._ Neither does;
they are for raw blocks and definition bodies respectively. Define a value and
refer to it by name to use it in prose. li@
@li _Overriding a variable outside the document's scope._ The theme scopes its
rules to the document class; an override outside that scope will not take. li@
@li _Reaching for raw when a tweak would do._ If you only need one colour changed,
a scoped override is far less work than styling from a reset. li@
ul@

